WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.932
I began the work that ultimately led to the

00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:25.032
sliding filament theory of muscle contraction in 1950.

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:36.032
I was a research student in a small MRC unit with John Kendrew as my supervisor

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:40.866
and Max Perutz being the head of the unit and that was it.

00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:43.466
It was just three people.

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:53.232
I'd been a student in physics, and my main interest had been in experimental particle physics.

00:00:53.233 --> 00:01:00.299
But, after graduation I decided to change fields completely, joined this unit,

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:07.199
and was in fact in complete freedom to choose my own particular subject of research.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.299
Now during the first couple of years I spent doing some work on hemoglobin

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.499
and learning a bit of biology of which I was totally ignorant originally.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:24.766
And then I chose muscle as a possible topic because

00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:28.766
I'd found that although an awful lot was known about the

00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:33.332
mechanics and energetics and biochemistry of muscle,

00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:37.866
virtually nothing was known about its submicroscopic structure

00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:41.766
nor about what actually happened during contraction.

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:46.932
Now, one way of getting that information might be

00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:55.666
that if muscle was composed of regular, repeating or semi-crystalline units of these

00:01:55.667 --> 00:02:04.399
two structural proteins then one might get evidence about what was happening from X-ray diffraction.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.099
However, people had looked at wide-angle X-ray diffraction,

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:14.432
which showed you any changes in the polypeptide chain configuration,

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:17.066
and essentially it didn't show anything.

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:23.666
And they'd also looked at dried muscle by low angle diffraction, which showed you

00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:31.132
structural regularities and the size of protein molecules, but that hadn't been informative either.

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:37.599
However, I'd learnt that one had to keep protein crystals fully hydrated

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.899
if you were going to get anything useful and an X-ray diagram from them.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.299
So I thought, "Well, maybe that would be true of muscle and

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:51.699
maybe muscle really did contain lots of regular structures if only one could attack it the right way."

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:57.999
And so for that purpose I built a miniaturized X-ray camera,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:08.399
which would optimize the sensitivity of images collected on film.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.166
And I was also very fortunate that my supervisor John Kendrew knew

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:20.099
a man called Bernal in London in whose X-ray group two people were

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:30.299
actually building a very fine-focus, high-intensity X-ray generator (X-ray tube) for another purpose,

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.066
and they were kind enough to give me an early prototype of this,

00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:39.266
which formed an essential part of the X-ray set-up that I built.

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:50.432
I was very thrilled and relieved to see that I did in fact get quite a well defined X-ray diagram.

00:03:50.433 --> 00:03:56.966
Not very detailed, but it consisted basically of two reflections.

00:03:56.967 --> 00:04:03.032
One here and then another one a bit further out.

00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:11.166
And, the spacing of these told one they came from a hexagonal arrangement of objects,

00:04:11.167 --> 00:04:17.699
and I concluded that this must be a hexagonal arrangement of myosin filaments.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.266
But, what was particularly interesting was that in a muscle in rigor,

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:26.232
the pattern had the same spacing, approximately,

00:04:26.233 --> 00:04:31.366
but the relative intensity of the two spots had changed,

00:04:31.367 --> 00:04:39.499
and this told one that there was some alteration in the character of the lattice.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:47.532
And that in fact you can get a projection of the electron density from those diagrams.

00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:54.099
This is a myosin filament here on my picture, and then this is another myosin filament there

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.899
and another one in the lattice over here.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:03.932
In between the myosin filaments is what I took to be actin filaments,

00:05:03.933 --> 00:05:10.266
which appeared at low density in the relaxed muscle, but in rigor muscle,

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:13.432
their density was much increased.

00:05:13.433 --> 00:05:21.466
Now I knew that actin and myosin tended to combine with others in the absence of ATP, that's in a rigor muscle.

00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:25.832
So I concluded, and it turned out I was correct, that

00:05:25.833 --> 00:05:36.632
was in fact what I was seeing: a double array of actin and myosin filaments interpenetrating with each other.

00:05:36.633 --> 00:05:43.099
I could also see some reflections, axial reflections, which give you information

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:47.999
about the longitudinal regularities along the length of the muscle.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:56.832
This is a spacing of about 145 angstroms, and there are another series of reflections not shown here.

00:05:56.833 --> 00:06:06.866
The interesting...so it showed that muscle was a sort of semi-crystalline structure at this scale.

00:06:06.867 --> 00:06:14.199
And these reflections didn't change in spacing when the muscle was passively stretched.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.666
Now, I made a mistake at that point.

00:06:17.667 --> 00:06:25.332
I thought that these reflections probably all came from the actin filaments.

00:06:25.333 --> 00:06:28.732
So I concluded the actin filaments didn't change in length,

00:06:28.733 --> 00:06:34.832
but I still had this picture that perhaps the myosin filaments somehow shortened down.

00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:41.232
Anyway, I finished my PhD and went to MIT to Schmitt's lab,

00:06:41.233 --> 00:06:47.099
which was a new center for doing electron microscope work,

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:53.432
because I wanted to make sure that this double array of filaments really existed.

00:06:53.433 --> 00:06:58.466
So by fixing, embedding and sectioning a muscle,

00:06:58.467 --> 00:07:03.499
I was able to look at cross sections in the electron microscope,

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:09.899
and lo and behold one could see the double array of filaments very, very clearly.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.932
This was a rabbit muscle in rigor.

00:07:12.933 --> 00:07:17.532
This is a myosin filament, and that's another myosin filament.

00:07:17.533 --> 00:07:25.999
And then the actin filaments are in this little ring of six of them around each myosin center.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:34.932
So that was very encouraging and then shortly after this, early in 1953,

00:07:34.933 --> 00:07:40.332
Jean Hanson came to the same lab to learn electron microscopy too

00:07:40.333 --> 00:07:47.599
because she'd been studying isolated myofibrils as seen in the light microscope.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.366
She was zoologist by training.

00:07:50.367 --> 00:07:55.166
And we decided, we were both doing the same thing essentially,

00:07:55.167 --> 00:08:01.832
we decided to combine our different approaches, and this worked out very successfully.

00:08:01.833 --> 00:08:04.999
One of the first experiments we did

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:11.999
was to look at myofibrils in the light microscope on a slide with a coverslip over them

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:19.499
in the phase contrast microscope, and you get very nice images of the A and I bands. I'll show you one in a moment.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:27.999
And when we irrigated these with solutions known to extract myosin from muscle,

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:38.799
we found that the dense material in the A band here was rapidly removed,

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:44.866
leaving a sort of ghost fibril which consisted of the original Z lines,

00:08:44.867 --> 00:08:53.199
and a segment of the filament which we concluded were probably actin.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:59.799
And we later found that applying actin extraction procedures

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:05.866
would remove this second set of filaments as well.

00:09:05.867 --> 00:09:11.699
These are just showing the density plots before and after myosin extraction.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:17.299
So, this was a sort of, quite a revolutionary finding for us.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:23.699
And after a few hours we sort of reoriented our way of thinking about things.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:31.999
And we realized that we were looking at two partially interpenetrating but overlapping arrays

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.899
of myosin filaments and actin filaments.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:43.766
And that the cross-sections I had seen had been through the overlap region here.

00:09:43.767 --> 00:09:49.766
So this is what I showed before-the overlap region.

00:09:49.767 --> 00:09:59.232
In the H zone, which is where the actin filaments have stopped, you just see the thick filaments.

00:09:59.233 --> 00:10:03.466
And in the I bands you can see the cross-section of the thin filaments.

00:10:03.467 --> 00:10:10.132
So we then had a quite new model of the structure of the muscle.

00:10:10.133 --> 00:10:15.366
And in between the two sets of filaments were these cross-bridges

00:10:15.367 --> 00:10:19.332
that I had postulated must be there to enable

00:10:19.333 --> 00:10:24.366
the two sets of the actin and myosin filaments to interact with each other.

00:10:24.367 --> 00:10:32.132
So we published a short paper on this in Nature, but at Schmitt's request

00:10:32.133 --> 00:10:37.666
we omitted any speculation about how this system would actually work.

00:10:37.667 --> 00:10:47.266
But what we knew was that there were these two axial series of X-ray reflections

00:10:47.267 --> 00:10:50.766
which didn't change during passive stretch.

00:10:50.767 --> 00:10:55.732
And so we thought, "Well maybe they didn't change during contraction either."

00:10:55.733 --> 00:11:01.999
And that would mean that the two sets of filaments would themselves remain constant in length

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:05.332
and that contraction would be achieved by

00:11:05.333 --> 00:11:10.799
the actin filaments sliding further into the array of myosin filaments.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:17.332
And when we...after a lot of hard work we were able to get sufficient images

00:11:17.333 --> 00:11:23.232
of muscle at different stages of contraction to prove that point.

00:11:23.233 --> 00:11:28.466
And you can see, this is a slightly stretched myofibril.

00:11:28.467 --> 00:11:35.399
Then, we give it a little bit of ATP, and it shortens down a little.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:41.099
The I bands, the clearer regions, become shorter, but the A bands stay constant in length.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:50.366
And as it shortens further still, the I bands get even shorter, and ultimately they almost disappear.

00:11:50.367 --> 00:11:58.032
We can also extract myosin from a stretched fibril,

00:11:58.033 --> 00:12:02.099
and we can see the large gap between the ends of the actin filaments.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:08.499
But when, on of course a different fibril, which is contracted, we can see

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:12.566
this gap between the actin shortening down and then disappearing.

00:12:12.567 --> 00:12:18.099
So we can measure the lengths of the I filaments and that was constant during contraction.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:26.766
So, this was written up for Nature and published in late May in 1954,

00:12:26.767 --> 00:12:33.666
along with another paper by Andrew Huxley, who's actually no relation of mine,

00:12:33.667 --> 00:12:41.366
and Niedergerke who had used a special interference microscope to look at intact fibers.

00:12:41.367 --> 00:12:46.799
And they'd found the same thing in so far as the A band stayed constant in length,

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:56.766
but their optics weren't such as to be able to see to measure the length of the actin filaments.

00:12:56.767 --> 00:13:05.232
So that we thought was pretty strong evidence for this type of model.

00:13:05.233 --> 00:13:12.499
But, it still remained quite controversial for a number of years

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:18.099
particularly because I think some of the other workers in the field

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:23.866
hadn't sort of got used to this combination of X-rays and EM and phase microscopy.

00:13:23.867 --> 00:13:31.432
But I think a few more people were probably convinced by some electron micrographs

00:13:31.433 --> 00:13:34.399
I was able to get two or three years later,

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:43.366
which showed the longitudinal sections through the myofibril, through the muscle fiber,

00:13:43.367 --> 00:13:49.632
in which you can see the thick filaments, very clearly I think,

00:13:49.633 --> 00:13:52.599
stopping at the end of the A band

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:58.632
whereas the thin filaments continue on into the I bands out here.

00:13:58.633 --> 00:14:03.966
And you can also, at higher magnification, you can see these cross-bridges

00:14:03.967 --> 00:14:07.699
between the actin and myosin filaments

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:16.499
that I'd supposed must be able to produce the relative sliding force that produces contraction.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:24.699
If there's any general conclusions about how you go about a problem you can draw from this,

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.999
well, I'm not sure that there are.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:31.032
I think I was very lucky because I had a clearly defined problem,

00:14:31.033 --> 00:14:37.932
and I was able to find some new techniques which provided a way into it.

00:14:37.933 --> 00:14:43.732
So, if you can repeat that process, you might get lucky like I did.

00:14:43.733 --> 01:00:00.000
Thank you.

